hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  in Chapter five of utilitarianism you  notice that you see John Stuart Mill  grappling with the notion of rights and  he talks about legal rights and moral  rights and you notice that he is  spending a good bit of discussion not  just analyzing the conception of what it  means to have a right but saying that  utilitarianism is going to by its very  workings honor what we think of as  rights now why is he so focused on that  a little bit of backstory his  predecessor Jeremy Bentham the original  articulator the originator in many  respects of what we call utilitarianism  understood in a quantitative sense had  said that rights were nonsense on stilts  that was the phrase that he used and  what he was talking about there were  legal rights whose enforcement turned  out to be bad for the community as a  whole to be bad for the aggregate of  people affected and this is still a  problem today when we see legal  decisions allowing certain persons or  corporations or the government to do  things which when we look at it we said  that's really bad policy or that's bad  decision-making because look at how it  affects everybody else the other  stakeholders so mill wants to say  utilitarianism is not going to end up  you know trampling on people's rights on  their legitimate rights and so how do we  understand rights it turns out there's  actually a couple different correlative  notions one of these is duty and another  one is the possibility of sanctioning  and he talks about this about midway  through this section so let's start with  the notion of duty he says that when we  think about what it means to have a duty  he says it's part of the notion in every  one of its forms that a person may  rightfully be compelled to fulfill it  Duty is the thing which may be exact it  from a person as one exacts a debt if  you have a duty to do X then somebody  should be able to tell you that you need  to do it it may not be legally  enforceable it might depend on what we  call public opinion or even as mill says  another possibility the person's own  conscience convicts him or her and says  you ought to be doing this when we could  think of all the different things that  mill talks about in terms of justice and  injustice like keeping promises treating  people and partially or in you know  equal in respects in which they are in  fact equal giving people what it is that  they deserve whether good or bad  honoring respecting rights all of those  things are possibly duties and mill says  that you can compel a person in one way  or another through some sort of sanction  to do that duty and he says by the way  we need to distinguish this from other  things there's other things which we  wish that people should do which we like  or admire them for doing or perhaps  dislike or despise them for not doing  but yet admit they're not bound to do  this is not a case of moral obligation  that is not a duty in any sort of strict  sense he says so you know I do have to  feed and clothe my children and I should  in fact look out for their welfare in  other respects but do I actually have to  show up at my kids recitals you know  because they invite me Mill would say no  there isn't actually a duty to do that  you know we're not going to call upon  the law to enforce that maybe your  conscience will bother you a bit but but  perhaps there's good reasons why you  don't make it to the to the concert of  the recital right whereas you know if  your kid is going without shoes or  medicine or not going to school there's  a real problem there there's some sort  of duty and duties have to do with not  doing her and some way injustice has to  do for Mill with harm so he goes on and  he tells us about this when we say that  a person has a right he says there there  are duties of per  but perfect obligation duties in virtue  of which a correlative right it resides  in some person or persons right so for  example if I have children the state  says that I need to educate them that  doesn't tell me exactly how I need to  educate them but it does say that they  need to be educated in some way they  could go to private school public school  may be homeschooled there could be all  sorts of different possibilities but I  do have to educate them and if I don't  do that I am violating their rights the  right to an education those all sorts of  other possible examples he talks about  you know depriving a person of a  possession breaking faith with them  treating them worse than they deserve or  worse than other people who have no  greater claims he says in each case the  supposition implies two things a wrong  and injustice that has done some  assignable person who is wronged  injustice mace has also be done by  treating a persons better than others  but the wrong is done in this case to  his competitors who are also persons who  have rights so he goes on and he says  that when we think about the notion of a  right residing in the injured person  what does that mean he says that it's  not a I've treated this not as a  separate element in the composition of  the idea and sentiment but as one of the  forms in which these these clothing each  other the elements are hurt to some  assignable person and a demand for  punishment society if we say that  somebody has a right we're saying that  society ought to enforce that right  society ought to punish or sanction in  some way the other person who has a duty  to respect that right now this could be  you know very singular I mentioned that  my children have a right to education  but somebody else's kid down the block  who has a right to education it's not  really my duty to provide them an  education is it it's my duty to provide  my children with an education  and if I if the neighbor if their kid  isn't going to school it's not like the  the social workers or you know Child  Protective Services are gonna come  knocking on my door to to sanction me  they're gonna sanction that guy down  there there are some things where we  have very very general rights right none  of us have the right to poison the water  supply that all of us drink from none of  us have the right to just you know light  a fire in a crowded theater or to yell  fire when there is no fire in a crowded  theater either as it turns out so  there's all sorts of things where you  know that maybe there's a whole bunch of  people have that duty or maybe that only  one person has that duty but society  ought to enforce that person's right  that's what it means to actually have a  right if I have a right to freedom that  means that if you try to kidnap me  somebody should step in whether it be  the police or whether it be in a public  sentiment or whether it be your own  conscience which probably won't be all  that effective if you are in fact  kidnapping me somebody ought to be  stepping in so Mill says that the idea  of the right includes a demand for  punishment he says these two things are  what we mean when we speak of violation  of a right we call anything a person's  right we mean that he has a valid claim  on society to protect him in the  possession of it either by the force of  law or that by by that of Education and  opinion if he has what we consider  sufficient claim to have something  guaranteed to him by society we say he  has a right to it if we desire to prove  that anything does not belong to him by  right  we think this done as soon as it's  admitted that society doesn't have to  take measures for securing it to him but  that he should provide it by his own  exertions so to use Mills previous  example I do have a right for example to  enter into economic competition with  other people and thereby to earn my  living I do not have a right to an  automatic one hundred thousand dollars a  year there's no you know society ought  to  provide that to me agreed upon by  everybody I there are some people who  think that everybody should get you know  a lot of money or something like that  but that's that's not agreed upon by the  totality of society so what is the  upshot of this for utilitarianism  utilitarianism when it's working well is  going to respect rights and it's going  to be involved in sanctioning well these  people who have duties to make sure  they're not violating other people's  rights notice to the last thing I want  to say that everybody not only is over  here but also over here we have  obligations towards each other by living  in society not only are you not supposed  to kill or defraud or slander me but I'm  not supposed to do that to you either so  we all have duties there are correlative  rights that correspond to those duties  there is a capacity for society to  sanction us in this this is the way the  utilitarians of Mills stripe do look at  rights so that they're not simply as  Bentham said nonsense on stilts  you
 

